vation\":{\"first_delivered_source_round\":null,\"prior_deliveries\":0,\"query_key\":\"74b00d15174506f75b5e27cf026e513f33074f9b1f5f87be643f71e6c5bd11eb\",\"replay_requested\":false,\"result_fingerprint\":\"106a5cced2bd059c3aada95d7c86b46bad9decc6c3f53ea03af00c196693247d\",\"semantic_progress\":\"not_measured\"},\"query\":\"Han Kang writer born where\",\"view\":\"full\"}]}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_f91b6a24bea6444590932dc6"},{"content":null,"role":"assistant","tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"goal\": \"Han Kang born Gwangju birthplace biography\", \"ref\": \"d33\"}","name":"read"},"id":"call_b3ea38b207274065af05c951","index":0,"type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"queries\": [\"Han Kang Wikipedia born Gwangju November 1970\", \"International Booker Prize 2016 shortlist six books\", \"Man Booker International 2016 shortlist A Whole Life Tram 83\"], \"top_k\": 10}","name":"search"},"id":"call_69ae1064fa564e8cac0c9201","index":1,"type":"function"}]},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"evidence\":{\"document\":\"d33\",\"document_chars\":5590,\"end\":5590,\"kind\":\"raw_evidence\",\"ref\":\"e1\",\"sha256\":\"1d57ba6fe1ec2500b762edaa594992659fe35a6130ff2876a3ab9ec0b1d2ad3d\",\"snapshot\":\"cf73f3198b61e06ea1409d0fa598f3ca5393950bb56774813f67f99bb8d90e1d\",\"start\":3585,\"text\":\"Han Kang composes this meditative, transcendental book while on a writers' residency in Warsaw. The white of the snow mixes with the white of memory.\\n\\nWriting becomes a purifying act: reconstructing her sister's death means starting to live. The resulting reflections follow the rhythm of prayer, perhaps a secular, yet deeply human prayer. The only way to mourn, and at the same time continue to live ethically, is the flash of memory, in its endless fragments.\\n\\nA nation's mourning\\n\\nIn Human Acts, this autobiographical mourning becomes the mourning of an entire nation.\\n\\nHuman Acts narrates the massacre in Gwangju, Kang's birthplace of May 1980, when hundreds, perhaps thousands, of citizens and university students, protesting against the authoritarian regime of South Korea's \\\"most vilified\\\" military dictator, Chun Doo-Hwan, were murdered by the army.\\n\\nParadoxically, precisely in the midst of these brutal acts what is most valuable emerges: solidarity, dignity, the strength to continue – and above all, the great responsibility of surviving and remembering.\\n\\n\\\"My novels explore human suffering,\\\" Han Kang once said. When she wrote about the Gwangju massacre, she was \\\"aware that readers should, in turn, be prepared […] to experience such suffering firsthand themselves\\\".\\n\\nThe ethical scope of the novel counteracts the collective amnesia imposed by censorship. It makes room for a chorale – a sacred song – in which the living are confused with the dead, the present with the past, memory with censorship, the word with the ineffability of a violence that is supposedly inhuman. Could surviving perhaps be a form of silent consent?\\n\\nBut as in The White Book, the silence crumbles in the writing, becoming too loud to bear. The role of the writer is to continue to create, remember, communicate even the incommunicable, despite it all. Even in shreds. Even silence. Even when humanity seems to fail us.\\n\\nFor this – and much more – Han Kang richly deserves this year's Nobel Prize for Literature.\"},\"executed\":true,\"next_start\":null,\"ok\":true,\"previously_received\":false,\"selection\":{\"block_truncated\":false,\"end\":5590,\"instruction\":\"Check the subject and adjoining context; lexical overlap does not establish the requested relation.\",\"matched\":true,\"matched_terms\":[\"kang\",\"gwangju\",\"birthplace\"],\"method\":\"paragraph-lexical-v1\",\"score\":5.347108,\"selected_block\":[4049,4445],\"selection_is_semantic_support\":false,\"start\":3585},\"title\":\"https://theconversation.com/my-novels-explore-human-suffering-nobel-prize-winner-han-kang-writes-with-empathy-for-vulnerable-lives-241064\"}","role":"tool","tool_call_id":"call_b3ea38b207274065af05c951"},{"content":"{\"action_slot_charged\":true,\"executed\":true,\"ok\":true,\"results\":[{\"cached\":false,\"hits\":[{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d66\"},\"ref\":\"d66\",\"snippet\":\" ... Maxim de Winter | Gwangju Cultural Center (formerly Gwangju Cultural and Art Center) | June 5 – 7, 2020 | |\\n| 2020 | 20th Anniversary of Werther | 베르테르 | Werther | Gwanglim Art Center BBCH Hall | September 1 - November 1 | |\\n| 2020–2021 | Monte Cristo | 몬테크리스토 | Edmond Dantes/Monte Cristo | LG Art Center | November 17, 2020 – March ... \",\"title\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Um_Ki-joon\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d33\"},\"ref\":\"d33\",\"snippet\":\" ... solidarity, dignity, the strength to continue – and above all, the great responsibility of surviving and remembering.\\n\\n\\\"My novels explore human suffering,\\\" Han Kang once said. When she wrote about the Gwangju massacre, she was \\\"aware that readers should, in turn, be prepared […] to experience such suffering firsthand themselves ... \",\"title\":\"https://theconversation.com/my-novels-explore-human-suffering-nobel-prize-winner-han-kang-writes-with-empathy-for-vulnerable-lives-241064\"},{\"kind\":\"navigation_not_evidence\",\"navigation_seen\":true,\"previously_received\":true,\"raw_ranges_delivered\":[],\"read_action\":{\"ref\":\"d67\"},\"ref\":\"d67\",\"snippet\":\" ... For example, in Every Smooth Thing Through Mesher (2018), shown at the Gwangju Biennale in 2018 and produced by the Hans Nefkens Foundation, he pinpoints the phenomenon of a perceiving the world through our smartphones, presenting the screen as a literal processor that confuses timelines of past, present ... \",\"title\":\"https://www